<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tayyibi Isma'ilism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tayyibi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a> is the branch of Musta'li Isma'ili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> that recognises al-Tayyib, the infant son of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Amir, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> after 1130. Backed by the Sulayhid queen Arwa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, it holds that the imam went into concealment and is represented by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai">da'i</a> al-mutlaq. Its leadership moved from Yemen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 16th century, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohras">Bohras</a> continue it.</p></div>
